WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.630
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.630 --> 00:00:09.930
In this series, we talk a lot about photorealistic
rendering, which is one of the most exciting

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:13.120
areas in computer graphics research.

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:17.640
Photorealistic rendering means that we put
virtual objects in a scene, assign material

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:23.460
models to them, and then run a light simulation
program to create a beautiful image.

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:27.670
This image depicts how these objects would
look like in reality.

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:33.170
This is particularly useful for the film industry
because we can create highly realistic scenes

00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:36.870
and set them up in a way that we couldn't
do in real life.

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:42.149
We can have any possible object we can imagine,
light sources that we wouldn't ever be able

00:00:42.149 --> 00:00:46.739
to buy, change the time of the day, or even
the planet we're on.

00:00:46.739 --> 00:00:49.239
Practically, we have an infinite budget.

00:00:49.239 --> 00:00:50.859
That's amazing!

00:00:50.859 --> 00:00:56.799
However, creating such an image takes a long
time, often in the order of hours to days.

00:00:56.799 --> 00:01:01.480
Here you can see me render an image and even
if the footage is sped up significantly, you

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:04.700
can see that this is going to take a long,
long time.

00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:11.009
Spp means samples per pixel, so this the number
of light rays we compute for every pixel.

00:01:11.009 --> 00:01:14.890
The more spp, the cleaner, more converged
image we get.

00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:18.150
This technique performs spatiotemporal filtering.

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:23.359
This means that we take a noisy input video
and try to eliminate the noise and try to

00:01:23.359 --> 00:01:25.750
guess how the final image would look like.

00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:31.140
And it can create almost fully converged images
from extremely noisy inputs.

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:37.090
Well, as you can see, these videos are created
with one sample per pixel, which is as noisy

00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:38.340
as it gets.

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:43.150
These images with the one sample per pixel
can be created extremely quickly, in less

00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:48.210
than ten milliseconds per image, and this
new denoiser also takes around 10 milliseconds

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:52.000
to reconstruct the final image from the noisy
input.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:56.380
And yes, you heard it right, this is finally
a real-time result.

00:01:56.380 --> 00:02:02.350
This all happens through decoupling the direct
and indirect effect of light sources and denoising

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:03.740
them separately.

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:08.360
In the meantime, the algorithm also tries
to estimate the amount of noise in different

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:13.130
parts of the image to provide more useful
information for the denoising routines.

00:02:13.130 --> 00:02:17.730
The fact that the entire pipeline runs on
the graphics card is a great testament to

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:19.890
the simplicity of this algorithm.

00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:24.370
Whenever you see the term SVGF, you'll see
the results of the new technique.

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:28.200
So we have these noisy input videos with 1
spp.

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:30.599
And...look at that!

00:02:30.599 --> 00:02:31.599
Wow!

00:02:31.599 --> 00:02:34.420
This is one of those papers that looks like
magic.

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:39.080
And, no neural networks or learning algorithms
have been used in this work.

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:45.780
Not so long ago, I speculated, or more accurately,
hoped that real-time photorealistic rendering

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:48.670
would be a possibility during my lifetime.

00:02:48.670 --> 00:02:51.790
And just a few years later, this paper appears.

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:56.720
We know that the rate of progress in computer
graphics research is just staggering, but

00:02:56.720 --> 00:02:59.140
this is, this is too much to handle.

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:03.650
Super excited to see where the artists will
take this, and of course, I'll be here to

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:05.739
show you the coolest followup works.

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:10.000
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

